description.narrative:
NaTA was founded in 2023, registered as a non-profit community-based organization in May, 2024 with Hoima local government, Uganda by a group of youth and University Students, majority are born and raised in Hoima district where the oil development activities are taking place in Uganda among other places. These young environment/land and human rights defenders, EHRDs and climate activists have been and are still witnessing the socio-economic injustices caused by the development of oil and gas in our communities. We believe “Real development must respect people's rights and conserve nature, the primary reasons we founded this organization is to contribute to the efforts to conserve our environment, develop sustainable community livelihoods in the oil host communities, develop and implement effective local climate actions and strengthen the people power on environmental and human rights for our local advocacyand holding the stakeholders accountable in any development activities.” Our mission is to contribute to resilient and sustainable communities where nature and people flourish and our vision is to provide a holistic innovative approach to community development, climate change adaptation, nature conservation, protection and preservation.
NatureTalk Africa Specific activities to the project;
● Supporting the ongoing legal cases (including the seven-community grassroots defenders case) and any other emerging legal issues directed to defenders and project affected persons (PAPs) in the Tilenga,EACOP and Kingfisher project areas in the district of Hoima, Kikuube and Buliisa. ● Organize two days’ training and capacity-building sessions on security risk assessments and management. During these sessions will also be given the opportunity to sharetheir experiences, strategies, successes, challenges, future plans to enable us to identify and formulate our updated community-specific security and protection plans. ● Organize one day training and capacity-building sessions on monitoring, documentationand reporting (MDR) of human rights violation to help in precise tracking and reporting of all the ongoing threats on EHRDs amidst oil and gas development in Uganda. ● Conduct legal empowerment training (compliance and application of UN human rights mechanisms) for 40 participants who will act as ambassadors to others in order to enable EHRDs to carry on their work with informed minds of their rights and duties as citizens.
